    "body": "The [chroot](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/7908799/xsh/chroot.html) API (Linux [man](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/chroot.2.html)) has no official \"chroot jail\" escape route but a typical sequence used to do so in the Unix world is\n\n```c\nchdir(\"/\");\nchroot(\"/path/to/jail\");\n// inside the new root\nchroot(\".\");\n// back at the normal root\n```\n\nThis works because `chroot` doesn't change the current directory, so a later `chroot` may go back outside of the jailed tree via \".\".\n\nThere are some OS/2 ports using this escape route. One of them is RPM (see https://github.com/bitwiseworks/rpm-os2/issues/15), but I believe there are more.\n\nIt's good to support such use cases.\n\nSee also #189.",
